    "title": "Applicant profile and next research pass",
    "body": "# Applicant profile and next research pass\n\nConfirmed: US master's research; Fall 2027; computer science and business; multiple ranking sources; costs, faculty orientation, internships and reusable scholarship research. Intended output is a general reference explaining university/program differences for prospective applicants. No budget or location filter.\n\nUser background: Hong Kong / Taiwan; computer science; internship experience. Exact legal citizenship/residency and extent of experience are not specified. Do not infer dual citizenship or scholarship eligibility.\n\nPersonal GPA, tests and financial limits are not prerequisites for this general research. Record admissions prerequisites and funding eligibility so different readers can assess themselves. Separate fresh-graduate, experienced-professional and research-oriented program fit.\n\nWorking business scope: specialized master's first, with MBA treated as a separate comparison category. This is a research organization choice, not a claim that the user selected a particular business specialization.\n\n## Next pass\n\n1. Organize CS master's by taught/professional/research structure and business master's by degree type and specialization.\n2. Build a broad candidate set using THE, US News and QS, retaining each source's edition/category separately. Add subject-specific research and accreditation sources.\n3. Check Fall 2027 availability, prerequisites and deadlines first; flag closed funding opportunities.\n4. Produce individual degree dossiers: costs, 3–5 relevant faculty where appropriate, curriculum/research orientation, internship evidence and eligible funding links.\n5. Explain differences in curriculum, faculty access, costs, internship structure, applicant prerequisites and career outcomes. Include a range of institutions rather than only elite-ranked schools. Keep personal application prioritization outside this general reference.\n\nNo applications, faculty outreach, emails or recurring monitoring have been initiated.\n"
